+## Worker ledger
+
+A successful launch appends exactly one `launched` record and prints
+`ledger: launch_id=<uuid>` on stderr; stdout stays the verbatim
+`agent_started` envelope. A launch that rolls back appends nothing, and
+`--dry-run` and `--json` write nothing. A ledger failure after a confirmed
+start prints `warning: worker started but ledger write failed: <reason>` and
+changes nothing else: the exit code stays `0` and the worker stays up.
+
+Record the worker's end only from evidence, never from scheduling state:
+
+```text
+brichan-herdr-worker-ledger finish \
+  --worker <brichan-name> \
+  --launch-id <uuid> \
+  --evidence <repo-relative-path> [--evidence <citation> ...] \
+  [--task <TASK-ID>] [--pane <pane-id>] \
+  --project <absolute-target-project>
+```
+
+`--project` names the target project root; when it is omitted, the Git root
+is discovered upward from the working directory. The target must be an
+initialized Brichan project — one holding a regular `.brichan/manifest.json`
+— or the command refuses with `no ledger for this target` and exit `1`.
+`finish` does not verify managed-state health: checking that the target is
+healthy before attesting stays the coordinator's responsibility.
+
+A `finished` record is a coordinator attestation, not proof: the command
+cannot verify completion, so it refuses a `--launch-id` that matches no
+`launched` record, a worker name without the `brichan-` prefix, and an empty
+or whitespace-only evidence item, each with exit `1` and nothing written.
+Each launch takes at most one attestation, and a second `finish` for the same
+`--launch-id` is refused; when two `finish` runs overlap, consumers take the
+first `finished` record for that `launch_id` in file order as the winning
+one. Absence of a `finished` record means "unknown", never "still running"
+or "failed".
+
+| Exit | Meaning |
+|---:|---|
+| `0` | Attestation recorded |
+| `1` | Refused or failed (no ledger, unmatched or already-finished `launch_id`, write failure) |
+| `2` | Invalid invocation |
+
